  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA cold front will progress through the area this afternoon and \nevening. Isolated showers and storms are possible ahead of the \nfront. A marginal risk for severe storms exists given fairly robust \ndeep layer shear (50+ knots winds aloft) and low to moderate \ninstability in place (~1000 J/kg MLCAPE). However, storm coverage \nappears to be on the lower end and shear is mostly straight line \nwithout much veering in the wind profile. Damaging wind gusts are \nthe most likely threat in any strong storms. The most likely area for\nstorms to occur is across central Ohio.\n\nThe shower and storm chances end this evening after FROPA. There \nwill be a noticeable drop in dew points behind the front as winds \nshift to the northwest tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nSoutherly flow redevelops across the Ohio Valley by Tuesday \nafternoon/evening after a warm front lifts through the region \nbringing the return of warm, humid conditions. Rain chances start to \nincrease Tuesday night north of I-70 and spread south through the \nend of the work week. A stationary front slowly sagging south likely \nwill act as a focus for rainfall. Zonal upper level flow could \nsupport weak shortwave troughs periodically increasing chances for \nrain through the week. \n\nTuesday likely features the most widespread heat before temperatures \nacross the north slowly moderate with the front slowly sinking into \nthe area. Areas south of the Ohio River may not experience FROPA \nuntil the weekend.",
